from _w import w
S=[
("Mobile County housing snapshot","""<p>Alabama's southwestern corner belongs to Mobile County, the state's third most populous, with 414,809 residents at the last full count. The city of Mobile is both seat and largest city. Coastline runs through the county, including Dauphin Island, Gaillard Island and Mon Louis Island, and it drives insurance costs, flood maps and house values.</p><p>The county has 163,750 households and 184,441 housing units, 11.2% of them vacant. Owners live in 64.6% of occupied homes, and 35.4% are rented. A good many sellers are landlords, and some rentals have unpaid tax behind them.</p><p>The county has nine cities: Bayou La Batre, Chickasaw, Citronelle, Creola, Mobile, Prichard, Saraland, Satsuma and Semmes. Dauphin Island and Mount Vernon are towns. Communities such as Theodore, Grand Bay, Tillmans Corner, Axis, Chunchula and Wilmer have no city government. A house in Saraland, one in Theodore and one on Dauphin Island all sit in one county, but the millage and school levy on each can differ, so confirm the taxing districts for your parcel before trusting an online estimate.</p>"""),
("Who handles property tax","""<p>Assessment and collection both sit with the Mobile County Revenue Commission. Every Wednesday its offices close, which trips people up in late December when lines are longest.</p><p>On a home sale, the Revenue Commission deals with value and billing, while the probate office records the deed. Do not mix up the two.</p><p>Landlords who supply appliances or furnishings, and anyone running a business with equipment at a house, face a personal property rule too. List it after October 1 and by December 31 at the latest. A listing filed past the third Monday in January draws a 10% penalty plus fees.</p>"""),
("Delinquency timeline","""<p>The tax falls due October 1 and is delinquent after December 31. The state timetable follows.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Month</th><th>Step in the state timetable</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>October 1</td><td>Taxes due</td></tr><tr><td>January 1</td><td>Taxes delinquent</td></tr><tr><td>February</td><td>Turned over to probate court</td></tr><tr><td>March</td><td>Probate court meets</td></tr><tr><td>April</td><td>Advertised for sale</td></tr><tr><td>May</td><td>Tax sale</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Anyone who knows a bill is unpaid has until the end of December to clear it before delinquency begins. Selling is easiest if you act ahead of January.</p>"""),
("Water and storms","""<p>Winters are mild and the air is humid. A 2007 study found about 66.3 inches of rain a year in Mobile, the most of any city in the contiguous 48 states. Inspectors look for water first, so roofs, drainage, crawl spaces and flood zones weigh heavily on a sale.</p><p>Category 3 Frederic crossed Mobile on September 12, 1979. Ivan hit on September 16, 2004. On August 29, 2005, Katrina pushed an 11.45 foot surge through downtown and the Battleship Parkway. Sally, in 2020, was the first hurricane since Ivan to strike Alabama head on, and a Category 3 Zeta came a month afterward.</p><p>Houses in eastern Mobile that flooded during Katrina, and any with storm repairs left half done, tend to stall on the open market. A cash buyer accepts them as they are.</p>"""),
("Appeals and closing","""<p>Put a valuation challenge in writing to the county Board of Equalization. Its ruling may be taken to circuit court within 30 days. Alabama Code 40-3-25 calls for notice to the board secretary and the circuit clerk and a bond, and an appeal can be dismissed if last year's tax was not paid before delinquency.</p><p>A title company or closing attorney generally takes any tax payoff out of the seller's proceeds at the table. The probate court records the deed, with a deed tax of $0.50 per $500 of value under Alabama Code 40-22-1.</p><ul><li>Request the current balance before you list or take an offer.</li><li>Check the parcel for exemptions that need to be released or reclaimed.</li><li>Gather the paperwork for any inherited property, since the deed chain must be clean.</li><li>Find out how the title company would treat a lien from an earlier year.</li></ul>"""),
("Who sells here","""<p>The typical county seller has a storm-worn roof, an aging house or an estate to settle. Many live in Mobile's historic neighborhoods, such as Ashland Place, Oakleigh Garden, Old Dauphin Way and Spring Hill, where upkeep costs more. Some hold houses in areas storms have hit before. Others inherited property and cannot cover repairs and back tax together.</p><p>Cash Home Buyers purchases Mobile County houses in their present state, tax due or not, so no repair money is needed before closing. The title company pays off unpaid property tax from the proceeds.</p>"""),
("What a cash sale skips","""<p>Selling to a buyer with a mortgage puts the house through an appraisal, a lender inspection and often an insurance check. In a county this wet, those steps expose trouble, from a worn roof to a damp crawl space, and each can lead to repair demands or a price cut.</p><p>None of that applies to a cash sale. The buyer sees the house as it is and prices it accordingly. Owners up against a deadline, whether a May tax sale, a move or an estate to wrap up, benefit most.</p>"""),
("Inherited and rental properties","""<p>Inherited houses are common here, and the tax account does not wait for the estate. If the property passed to several heirs, work out who is the owner of record and who has authority to sign before anything is listed. The title company will not close until the deed chain is clean, and an open question about an heir can add weeks.</p><p>For a rental, check whether personal property such as appliances or furnishings has been listed on time. A missed listing deadline carries a penalty, and that balance can show up on the payoff. Ask the Revenue Commission for the full picture, real and personal, so nothing surprises you at the closing table.</p><p>Where a tenant lives in the house, tell the tenant early and keep showings polite and scheduled. A cooperative tenant makes a quick sale far easier, and a cash buyer can often take a house with the lease in place.</p>"""),
("Steps from first call to closing","""<p>Step one is learning the balance: call the Revenue Commission, on any day but Wednesday, and ask about the amount owed and any earlier open year. Step two is collecting the deed and estate papers. Step three is requesting a written cash offer and comparing what you would net once the tax is paid.</p><p>Step four is picking a closing date with room for the title search. The title company verifies ownership, requests the payoff, drafts the settlement statement and arranges recording. You sign once, the tax is paid out of the proceeds, and the remainder goes to you.</p><p>For a rental, put the leases in the folder and decide whether the tenants will stay. The buyer will ask before the offer is final.</p><p>Finally, keep the payoff letter, the settlement statement and the recorded deed together after closing. They are your proof that the tax account was cleared.</p>"""),
]
F=[("When is Mobile County property tax due?","It is due October 1 and becomes delinquent after December 31, and the Mobile County Revenue Commission does the collecting."),
("Is the Mobile County Revenue Commission open on Wednesdays?","No. Every office closes on Wednesdays, so go on another weekday."),
("What happens if I do not pay Mobile County property tax?","The bill turns delinquent after December 31. Probate court gets it in February, notices run in April, and a tax sale happens in May."),
("How long do I have to appeal an assessment in Mobile County?","Start with a written appeal to the Board of Equalization. To take the board's decision to circuit court, you have 30 days under Alabama Code 40-3-25."),
("Can I sell a Mobile County house that has back property tax?","Yes. Proceeds at closing cover the back tax through the title company. Get the balance from the Revenue Commission before you start.")]
w('mobile-county-al-property-tax-guide',S,F)
